6. Display PCA data
print("\nPCA Data:")print(x_pca)



Explanation:
- print() – Displays the output.
- x_pca – Contains the transformed data with two columns (PC1 and PC2).
- Each row represents one wine sample.
Purpose: To display the reduced dataset after applying PCA.
7. Explained variance ratio
print("\nExplained Variance Ratio:")print(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)



Explanation:
- pca.explained_variance_ratio_ – Gives the proportion of total variance explained by each principal component.
- First value – Variance explained by PC1.
- Second value – Variance explained by PC2.
Example output:
[0.36 0.19]


This means:
- PC1 explains 36% of the variance.
- PC2 explains 19% of the variance.
- Together, the two components explain 55% of the variance.
The actual values depend on your dataset.
8. Visualize PCA
plt.scatter(x_pca[:, 0], x_pca[:, 1], c=y)plt.xlabel("Principal Component 1")plt.ylabel("Principal Component 2")plt.title("PCA - Wine Dataset")plt.show()



Explanation:
- plt.scatter() – Creates a scatter plot.
- x_pca[:, 0] – Selects the first principal component for the X-axis.
- x_pca[:, 1] – Selects the second principal component for the Y-axis.
- c=y – Uses target class labels to assign different colors to wine classes.
- plt.xlabel() – Labels the X-axis.
- plt.ylabel() – Labels the Y-axis.
- plt.title() – Adds the graph title.
- plt.show() – Displays the graph.
Purpose: To visualize the wine samples in two dimensions and observe how different classes are distributed.

